#!/bin/sh -vx
# $Id$
# Public domain. tftopl should complain about a bad tfm if TeX does.
# From doogwoo, 30jul26, https://tug.org/pipermail/tex-k/2026-July/004373.html.
# (original posted message copied below).

BinDir=${BinDir:-.}
ExeExt=${ExeExt:-}
_tex=$BinDir/tex$ExeExt
_tftopl=$BinDir/tftopl$ExeExt

# in case we're invoked standalone instead of from make.
test -z "$srcdir" && srcdir=`cd \`dirname $0\`/.. && pwd` # web2c/
TEXMFCNF=$srcdir/../kpathsea; export TEXMFCNF

badfont=$srcdir/tests/fontgap-bad.tfm

buggy=0

# A particular bad tfm constructed with the steps below.
# Both TeX and tftopl should complain.
if $_tex -ini '\nonstopmode\font\x='$badfont' \relax' </dev/null; then
  echo "$0: TeX should not have succeeded."
  buggy=1
fi

if $_tftopl $badfont fontgap-bad.pl; then
  echo "$0: tftopl should not have succeeded."
  buggy=2
fi

exit $buggy


Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 23:10:30 -0700
From: <doogwoo@gmail.com>
To: <tex-k@tug.org>
Subject: TFtoPL 3.3 misses invalid char_info fields in nonexistent character slots

[unfortunately missing from online tex-k mailing list archive. --karl]

I found a TFM file that TeX rejects, while TFtoPL accepts the same file
without any diagnostic. That seems inconsistent with the guarantees in the
source documentation for both programs.

Versions, all as shipped in TeX Live 2026:

  TeX 3.141592653
  TFtoPL 3.3
  PLtoTF 3.6
  kpathsea 6.4.2


What the programs promise
-------------------------

tex.web module 561 explains why TeX's own message is so terse:

  There are programs called TFtoPL and PLtoTF that convert between the TFM
  format and a symbolic property-list format that can be easily edited. These
  programs contain extensive diagnostic information, so TeX does not have to
  bother giving precise details about why it rejects a particular TFM file.

tftopl.web module 1 makes the corresponding promise:

  It also makes a thorough check of the given TFM file, using essentially the
  same algorithm as TeX. Thus if TeX complains that a TFM file is "bad," this
  program will pinpoint the source or sources of badness.

The section titled "Doing it" states the same goal more directly:

  Of course, a bad file shows up every now and again, and that's where TFtoPL
  comes in. This program wants to catch at least as many errors as TeX does,
  and to give informative error messages besides.


Reproduction
------------

Build a font with a gap in its character range:

  $ cat > gap.pl <<'EOF'
  (FAMILY GAPTEST)
  (DESIGNSIZE R 10.0)
  (CHARACTER C A (CHARWD R 0.5))
  (CHARACTER C E (CHARWD R 0.25))
  EOF
  $ pltotf gap.pl gap.tfm

That gives bc=65, ec=69, ni=1, and zero char_info words for characters 66,
67, and 68. A zero width index means that the character does not exist in the
font. The char_info word for character 66 starts at byte 96 + 4*(66-65) =
100, so byte 102 contains italic_index<<2 | tag. Set italic_index to 63,
which is well past ni=1:

  $ python3 -c "b=bytearray(open('gap.tfm','rb').read()); b[102]=252; \
      open('gap-bad.tfm','wb').write(bytes(b))"
  $ cmp -l gap.tfm gap-bad.tfm
  103   0 374

This changes one byte in a character slot that does not exist.

TeX rejects it:

  ! Font \f=gap-bad not loadable: Bad metric (TFM) file.

TFtoPL accepts it without printing anything:

  $ tftopl gap-bad.tfm gap-bad.pl
  $ echo $?
  0
  $ grep -c 'THE TFM FILE WAS BAD' gap-bad.pl
  0

There is no output on stdout or stderr, the exit status is 0, and the PL file
contains no correction marker. The -verbose option does not change this; the
help text describes it as a way to display progress reports.

If the same corruption is applied to an existing character, TFtoPL behaves as
documented:

  Italic correction index for character '101 is too large;
  so I reset it to zero.

The resulting PL file also contains the usual
`(COMMENT THE TFM FILE WAS BAD, SO THE DATA HAS BEEN CHANGED!).`


Where the two readers differ
----------------------------

tex.web module 569, in the section named "Read character data," walks the
whole char_info array without checking whether the character exists:

  for k:=fmem_ptr to width_base[f]-1 do
    begin store_four_quarters(font_info[k].qqqq);
    if (a>=nw)or(b div @'20>=nh)or(b mod @'20>=nd)or
      (c div 4>=ni) then abort;
    case c mod 4 of
    lig_tag: if d>=nl then abort;
    ext_tag: if d>=ne then abort;
    list_tag: @<Check for charlist cycle@>;

tftopl.web module 78, on the other hand, wraps the character-output block in
`width_index(c)>0`:

  for c:=bc to ec do if width_index(c)>0 then

As a result, the height, depth, italic-correction, extensible-index, and
charlist checks inside that block do not run for a nonexistent character. The
change file does not alter this condition.

There is one exception worth pointing out. TFtoPL checks ligature starting
indices separately while it builds its label table. Therefore an invalid
lig_tag remainder is reported and reset rather than silently accepted.

Using logo10.tfm, which has bc=65, ec=84, and an absent character 66, the
results are:

  field pushed out of range      tex       tftopl
  ---------------------------    reject    --------
  height_index = 15 (nh=2)       reject    silent
  depth_index = 15 (nd=1)        reject    silent
  italic_index = 16 (ni=2)       reject    silent
  ext_tag remainder = 250 (ne=0) reject    silent
  lig_tag remainder = 250 (nl=3) reject    reports and resets

The same fields on a character that exists are reported by TFtoPL.

For the italic corruption shown above, TFtoPL reads the file without complaint
and omits the malformed slot from the PL output. Passing that PL through PLtoTF
therefore removes the bad data:

  $ pltotf gap-bad.pl gap-laundered.tfm

The resulting TFM file loads successfully. A user whose font fails to load can
therefore round-trip it through TFtoPL and PLtoTF, get a working font, and lose
the only evidence of what was wrong with the original file.


What should change
------------------

The direct fix would be to validate the relevant index fields before the
`width_index(c)>0` test, while still omitting nonexistent characters from the
PL output.

There is a broader question about which reader should change. The format
description says that a character is valid if and only if it lies between bc
and ec and has a nonzero width_index. It does not explicitly constrain the
other index fields or the tag of an absent slot. One could therefore argue
that TeX is stricter than the format requires and that tex.web should accept
these fields instead. Either way, the current implementations disagree, and
the promise in tftopl.web is not true as written.

Item 426 in tex82.bug went in the other direction, adding a check to TeX
because TFtoPL already performed it:

  426. For robustness, enforce a restriction checked by TFtoPL (suggested by
  DRF)

I could not find an erratum covering the reverse inconsistency.

LuaTeX 1.24.0 currently agrees with TFtoPL and loads gap-bad.tfm. Its reader
has the same width_index gate in luatexdir/font/tfmofm.c. pdfTeX and XeTeX
reject the file, like TeX. I have sent a separate note about that to
dev-luatex, since either resolution will affect LuaTeX as well.

